  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Saturday)\nIssued at 1155 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nA thick stratus deck and northwest winds have prohibited much\nwarming this Sunday morning. Temperatures as of 11 AM are in the\n70s across the entire Mid-South. Satellite imagery shows a few\ncloud breaks emerging, which will support minor warming; however,\nmost of the area will remain below 80F. The most likely area to\nreach 80 degrees or slightly above is areas along and west of the\nMississippi River. Light drizzle and sprinkles have been observed\neast of the Mississippi River, but some isolated chances for\nshowers in this same vicinity cannot be ruled out this afternoon\nand evening as troughing continues over the southeast. As the\ntrough continues its path, expect a similar forecast tomorrow\nwith warmer temperatures in western zones and cooler temperatures\nwith isolated rain chances in the east.\n\nThe base of the trough will finally depart by Tuesday, but an\nupper level cut-off low looks to emerge over the southeast. On\nthe back side of this system, a gradual warm up will occur with\nsurface high pressure. Temperatures will begin to return to\nnormal beginning in eastern Arkansas, reaching the lower 90s. By\nmid-to-late week, southerly flow returns, supporting isolated\ndiurnally driven thunderstorms. However, convection may struggle\nagainst dry air aloft. Thankfully, the southerly flow will start\nout rather weak and moisture transport will be modest. However,\nby the weekend, the flow amplifies and heat index values are\nprobable to return into the triple digits. There are some low to\nmedium probabilities of heat index values nearing 105F as early\nas Friday, but overall, guidance has trended a touch cooler.\nEnjoy the cooler temperatures in the short term, but be prepared\nfor heat and humidity to return next weekend.\n\nDNM",
